TI  - Greenwashing perception and attitude-intention relationship towards green products purchase: a mediating model
AU  - Gupta, K
AU  - Singh, N
T2  - ENVIRONMENT DEVELOPMENT AND SUSTAINABILITY
AB  - Purpose: This study explores how consumer attitudes towards green products mediate the connection between 'greenwashing perception' and 'intention to purchase green products.' This research provides significant insights and recommendations for policymakers, managers, and consumers to achieve the Sustainable Development Goal (S.D.G.) No. 12: Sustainable consumption and production. Design/methodology/approach: This research employs descriptive methodology to explore how customers react to greenwashing practices within the 'fast-moving consumer goods' (FMCG) sector. Utilizing a questionnaire administered through an online platform, data was gathered and subsequently analyzed through the Smart P.L.S. software package to confirm the suggested hypotheses. Findings: The results show that Attitude toward green products partially mediates the relationship between 'greenwashing perception' and 'green purchase intention.' The results indicate that misleading and false claims by marketers adversely affect customers' attitudes toward green products, thus developing greenwashing perception of consumers. Practical implications: This study holds significant implications for marketers, highlighting the impact of Attitude toward green products on organizations' growth. Policymakers have the potential to make a meaningful impact by enacting regulatory reforms aimed at curtailing greenwashed practices. Furthermore, customers can enhance their awareness of unethical marketing tactics and make well-informed choices when navigating the marketplace. Originality/value: This research studies the gap, identifying the role of Consumer attitude as a mediator between greenwashing perception and consumers' green purchase intention. Thus, it identifies the role of consumers' green attitudes in sustainable consumption.
