TY  - CONF
TI  - FUTURE SCENARIOS FOR TEACHING AND LEARNING: DEVELOPING STUDENT CAPABILITIES IN THE ERA OF POST TRUTH AND FAKE NEWS
AU  - McLoughlin, C
T2  - 12TH INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE OF EDUCATION, RESEARCH AND INNOVATION (ICERI2019)
A2  - Chova, LG
A2  - Martinez, AL
A2  - Torres, IC
AB  - Among the challenges facing higher education and threatening the credibility of knowledge is the rise of fake news, fake speech, distortion of facts and the use of 'strategic persuasion' by media and newspapers, intentionally and otherwise. The post truth age coincides with the rise of social media, contributing to the present overheated information environment in which various forms of fake news, compete for our attention. The volume of distorted facts undermines the credibility of real news and authentic facts which leads to doubts about the credibility of real data. The purpose of this scoping review is to provide an overview of relevant research on the impact of the post truth movement and fake news in higher education, curriculum and pedagogy. The central question underpinning the review is to ask to investigate how academics in higher education can fight back in the age of information warfare, defend the truth and teach students to seek and validate it though a range of literacies. The results indicate that essential media, information and literacy skills are essential, for example how to fact check, detect bias, identify bigotry and evaluate false claims. The creation of holistic meaningful interdisciplinary curriculum frameworks and critical literacies that that go beyond knowledge recall are essential starting points. Results of the scoping review indicate that development of students as reflective, critical consumers and creators of knowledge is paramount. The conclusion will outline current responses to the threats of the post truth era, by presenting pedagogies and frameworks for digital literacies that foster essential skills to navigate the current information ecosystem. Additional areas for research are also identified.
