TI  - Classifying True and Fake News using LSTM Architecture
AU  - Kant, V
AU  - IEEE
T2  - 2024 SECOND INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON INTELLIGENT CYBER PHYSICAL SYSTEMS AND INTERNET OF THINGS, ICOICI 2024
AB  - The explosion of knowledge available on digital channels has transformed news distribution and consumption. But this has also resulted in the proliferation of false news, so seriously impairing public debate, public confidence, and the Sustainable Development Goals on peace, justice, and solid institutions as well as on quality education and peace. Maintaining journalistic integrity and informed public discourse depends on one being able to separate fact from fiction. Leveraging developments in machine learning, this work uses Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to identify news items as real or false. Because LSTMs can capture dependencies and patterns in sequential text data, they are very successful for this work. Proposed LSTM model makes use of fact-checking tools and a collection of labeled news items from credible sources. Strong accuracy of the model is shown by thorough experimental and validation procedures; a 97.90% success rate in differentiating real from fraudulent news is obtained. This work seeks to improve automated systems for countering false information, so improving media literacy, social confidence, and accurate information distribution. This work addresses the important demand for accurate information in the digital era by offering a scalable approach, therefore augmenting the already increasing body of knowledge in automated fake news detection.
