TI  - Overcoming structural and similarity barriers: Graph neural network-based contextual reasoning for detecting incongruent and partially misleading news
AU  - Kumar, S
AU  - Kumar, S
AU  - Singh, SR
T2  - NEUROCOMPUTING
AB  - Detecting incongruent news has become a crucial research challenge in combating the spread of misinformation and disinformation across digital platforms and mitigating its impacts on readers. Initial studies on incongru ent news detection consider bag-of-words-based features to estimate the similarity between news headlines and bodies. Further studies on incongruent news detection mainly rely on estimating the similarity between news headlines and news bodies or the similarity between news headlines and synthetically generated headlines by summarizing news bodies. However, bag-of-words methods struggle to capture deep semantics, complex nega tions, and propositional content within and between the news body and headline. Similarly, similarity-based approaches face challenges with length mismatches, and both similarity-and summarization-based methods are ineffective in detecting partially incongruent news with partial truths and partially misleading elements. Both similarity-and summarization-based methods also fail when there is a topic or domain mismatch between datasets, or dissimilarities between the headline and news body, or within segments of the news body. Motivated by the above limitations, this study proposes the Graph-based Context Matching (GCM) method, which first rep resents the news headline and body in the form of n-gram networks and then extracts the context of each word in the headline from the different segments of the news body and then estimates the similarity between the extracted context from the news body and headline for incongruent news detection. Our experimental findings across five publicly available benchmark datasets indicate that the proposed GCM model outperformed stateof-the-art models from the literature and is highly proficient in detecting incongruent news articles of diverse categories and characteristics, including incongruent news, incongruent news in the form of fake news articles, partially incongruent news in the form of partial-truths and partial-misleading news articles, deceptive news in the form of catchy headlines and clickbait. Additionally, since our proposed method does not rely on direct com parison between the encoded representations of the headline and the full news body, it effectively overcomes the text length mismatch and structural limitations present in similarity-based and summarization-based studies in the literature.
