TI  - Inoculation Strategies for Misinformation Resistance: A Multidimensional Intervention Approach
AU  - Yang, YT
AU  - Hu, W
AU  - Zhou, J
AU  - Xiang, HL
T2  - INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION
AB  - The widespread application of artificial intelligence-generated content (AIGC) poses a risk of spreading misinformation. Existing studies have proposed using inoculation theory to reduce misinformation acceptance through preventive interventions. However, the most suitable inoculation strategies for different age groups remain unclear. This study designed a card-based tabletop prototype and developed the online game version. We recruited 80 participants to examine the effects of age (younger and older adults), interaction levels (game-based and text-based), and inoculation forms (active and passive inoculation) on attitude changes toward misinformation. The results indicated active inoculation is more effective than passive inoculation at text-based interaction for younger adults to resist misinformation. Under passive inoculation, game-based interaction is more effective than text-based interaction for older adults. These findings extend inoculation theory and provide insights for developing accurate misinformation intervention strategies for different age groups in the AIGC era.
