AB  - The main objective of the research outlined in this paper is to understand how higher education students who are attending a Media and Communication bachelor's degree perceive some aspects related to fake news and online disinformation. To conduct this case study, information was collected using an online questionnaire and a focus group. In this paper, we present the results collected from the focus group and that cover several questions related to student's perceptions of fake news and online disinformation. The sample was composed of eight students who were attending the 2nd year of a bachelor's degree in Media and Communication in 2018/2019. The results emphasize that students are familiar with fake news and with what seems to motivate people to create and disseminate them. They are also aware of some specific characteristics of this type of news and can identify websites that usually publish false stories. Participants reveal that most of the manipulated news they have found are released on social media and suggest that the news and information they trust most are those they can get from printed media. The results obtained advocate that students are aware of the negative impact of the phenomenon and that education is the best way to prevent the dissemination of fake news
