TI  - Combating distorted information through third person perception advances in a meta-analysis
AU  - Li, XY
AU  - Lyu, W
AU  - Salleh, SM
AU  - Ahmad, AL
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - This meta-analysis synthesizes evidence on the third-person effect in the domain of distorted information, addressing both perceptual and behavioral components. A systematic search of seven databases identified 30 relevant articles. Perceptual outcomes (k = 44) and diverse behavioral outcomes with sufficient study counts (k >= 3) were meta-analyzed, while publication bias and heterogeneity tests were applied to ensure robustness. Results show that distorted information elicited a strong third-person perception (TPP) (r = 0.634). For behavioral outcomes, TPP significantly predicted corrective action (r = 0.158) and was negatively associated with sharing intention (r = - 0.296) but did not predict regulatory support (r = 0.158, p = 0.211). By contrast, presumed media effect on others (PME3) (r = 0.240) and presumed media effect on self (PME1) (r = 0.166) significantly predicted regulatory support, suggesting that governance-oriented responses derive more from generalized vulnerability than from relative self-other gaps. Moderator analyses revealed variations across topics (e.g., health, politics, environment, general) and terminology (misinformation, disinformation, fake news, false news, rumors), though these differences may partly reflect methodological inconsistencies. By analyzing both perceptual outcomes and behavioral responses, this study provides a more comprehensive account of how distorted information shapes both self-other perceptual gaps and subsequent behavioral responses.
