TY  - JOUR
TI  - "The facts speak for themselves": dismantling conspiracy theories as disinformation
AU  - Sousa-Silva, R
T2  - LINGUISTICS VANGUARD
AB  - Disinformation has been commonly approached as fake news, i.e., news that does not comply with the principles of factuality, objectivity, and neutrality. However, not all pieces of disinformation are damaging (e.g., satire) or rely on lack of factuality. Rather, it is the combination of lack of factuality and intention to deceive that embodies the most serious form of disinformation. By taking advantage of echo chambers and filter bubbles, disinformers use distorted facts to disseminate alternative forms of disinformation and manipulate readers' views. This article discusses the relevance of establishing a sociolect of conspiracy theories (CTs) as alternative sources of disinformation. It builds on a small corpus of CTs published in Portuguese to explore the use of forensic linguistics methods to assist the detection of disinformation. A holistic linguistics approach is adopted, which operates by scrutinizing metadata, structure, and discourse to identify which linguistic features depart from mainstream sources and which ones overlap, and thus understanding the linguistic materializations of CTs. The findings reveal promising results in detecting CTs as alternative sources of disinformation, with the additional advantage of substantiating judgements of disinformation with linguistic evidence. This article concludes with a discussion of the limitations of this exploratory research.
O conceito de desinforma & ccedil;& atilde;o tem sido abordado como sin & oacute;nimo de not & iacute;cias falsas, i.e. not & iacute;cias que n & atilde;o cumprem os princ & iacute;pios de factualidade, objetividade e neutralidade. Por & eacute;m, nem toda a desinforma & ccedil;& atilde;o & eacute; prejudicial (e.g., a s & aacute;tira), nem viola a factualidade. & Eacute; a combina & ccedil;& atilde;o de falta de factualidade e inten & ccedil;& atilde;o de enganar que constitui a forma mais grave de desinforma & ccedil;& atilde;o. Aproveitando c & acirc;maras de eco e bolhas de filtro, os desinformadores distorcem factos para disseminar formas alternativas de desinforma & ccedil;& atilde;o e manipular a opini & atilde;o dos leitores. Este artigo discute a relev & acirc;ncia de um sociolecto de teorias da conspira & ccedil;& atilde;o (TC), enquanto formas alternativas de desinforma & ccedil;& atilde;o, partindo da an & aacute;lise de um pequeno corpus de TC publicadas em portugu & ecirc;s para estudar a utiliza & ccedil;& atilde;o de m & eacute;todos de lingu & iacute;stica forense na sua dete & ccedil;& atilde;o. A investiga & ccedil;& atilde;o adota uma abordagem lingu & iacute;stica hol & iacute;stica, baseada na an & aacute;lise de metadados, estrutura e discurso, para identificar quais os tra & ccedil;os lingu & iacute;sticos que divergem das fontes convencionais e quais aqueles que se sobrep & otilde;em, procurando assim compreender as materializa & ccedil;& otilde;es lingu & iacute;sticas das TC. Os resultados revelam-se promissores, quer na dete & ccedil;& atilde;o de TC como fontes alternativas de desinforma & ccedil;& atilde;o, quer na fundamenta & ccedil;& atilde;o de ju & iacute;zos sobre desinforma & ccedil;& atilde;o com base em prova lingu & iacute;stica. O artigo conclui com uma discuss & atilde;o das limita & ccedil;& otilde;es deste estudo explorat & oacute;rio.
