TI  - Responses to contested information amid polarized politics: evidence from the US and Taiwan
AU  - Radnitz, S
AU  - Hsiao, Y
T2  - INFORMATION COMMUNICATION & SOCIETY
AB  - People increasingly encounter news in politicized spaces online, where various actors contest the veracity of information. This paper assesses how the context surrounding claims of disinformation shapes people's beliefs. We conducted original survey experiments in the US and Taiwan, randomly varying the identities of the actors alleging disinformation and those supposedly spreading disinformation. Our framework advances on previous work by incorporating the logics of both information correction and the weaponization of mis/disinformation. We find that disinformation claims exert significant downward effects on belief, whether the information is true, false, or indeterminate. Disinformation allegedly spread by foreign actors elicits higher discounting than by domestic partisan operatives. Overall, we find that respondents' judgments are shaped more by prevailing discourses about disinformation than by the information's content or strictly partisan motivations. The study emphasizes how discourses about the disinformation 'threat' can negatively affect public trust in information, with troubling implications for democracy.
