TI  - From Twitter to X: origins, disinformation and the exodus of prestige media. A case study of The Guardian and La Vanguardia media
AU  - Díaz, JB
AU  - Vicario, LM
AU  - Sobrepere, MP
T2  - HIPERTEXT NET
AB  - Since its inception, Twitter established itself as a key tool for public debate and the dissemination of information in real time, especially during high-impact events such as natural disasters, social movements, and political cycles originating around the world. Following Elon Musk's acquisition of Twitter in 2022, significant changes were introduced to its business model and management, which has intensified disinformation and altered the dynamics of the platform's use, as well as its algorithms, which ensure interaction and monetization. All these changes have led institutions and media to abandon the social network, the most notable case being that of the British newspaper The Guardian in November 2024, followed by La Vanguardia in the same month, coinciding with Donald Trump's victory in the 2024 presidential elections. Our study analyzes how Musk's management has dismantled the methods intended to prevent the dissemination of fake news, instead fostering an increase in disinformation and several of its side effects such as hate speech. Using a mixed approach, we seek to identify whether this phenomenon is the main factor behind the decision of The Guardian and La Vanguardia, or whether there are other underlying reasons related to the current policies and dynamics of the platform. The results provide a necessary critical analysis of the deviation from the original purpose of the social network and its ability to condition public debate to the point of influencing the future of entire nations.
