TI  - Disinformation in emergency situations: a case study of fact-checking agencies during the DANA in Spain
AU  - López-Marcos, C
AU  - Vicente-Fernández, P
AU  - Hidalgo-Cobo, P
T2  - REVISTA MEDITERRANEA COMUNICACION-JOURNAL OF COMMUNICATION
AB  - Purpose. This study examines how Spanish fact-checking agencies tackled disinformation during the 2024 DANA event in Spain, assessing their effectiveness in managing false narratives in emergency contexts. Methodology. A quantitative, exploratory, and descriptive study was conducted through content analysis (n = 438) using a customized coding system applied to three IFCN-affiliated agencies. Results and Conclusions. Findings reveal social media as the main vector of disinformation and highlight varied agency strategies: Maldita and EFE Verifica prioritized debunking falsehoods, while Newtral emphasized explanatory content. Misleading information was the most frequent type, with limited presence of conspiracy theories or hate speech. Original contribution. This study provides an in-depth view of how fact-checkers operate during real-time disinformation outbreaks in emergencies, offering empirical insights into their role in crisis communication management.
